THE LONG GAME | No. 98 | March 17, 2026

What Thucydides Doesn't Tell You

The "Thucydides trap", the claim that a rising power and an established one t=
end toward war, has become the default frame for U.S.-China relations. It is =
a useful warning and a poor model.

It's useful because it names a real mechanism: fear. The established power re=
ads every gain by the riser as a threat, the riser reads every precaution as =
encirclement, and each side's defensive moves confirm the other's worst assum=
ptions. Anyone who has watched the last decade of tariff and export-control e=
scalation has seen that spiral.

It's a poor model for three reasons. First, the historical sample is small an=
d contested; scholars can't agree on how many of the canonical cases fit. Sec=
ond, nuclear weapons change the payoff of a direct war so much that the compa=
rison with Athens and Sparta mostly breaks. Third, and most important, the tr=
ap is described as structural, which invites fatalism. Structure sets the pre=
ssures; it doesn't make the decisions.

A more useful frame is the security dilemma plus domestic politics. Leaders i=
n both capitals face audiences that reward toughness and punish visible conce=
ssions. The dangerous moments are therefore less likely to be grand strategic=
 choices than small incidents (a collision at sea, a detained crew) that lead=
ers can't be seen to back down from.

What lowers the risk in practice:
- Crisis communication channels that work at the operational level, not just =
between presidents.
- Rules for unplanned encounters at sea and in the air, actually practiced.
- Economic interdependence in areas neither side wants to lose, which is shri=
nking.

History rhymes. It doesn't decide.

